| Claim | Assessment |
|---|---|
| **2.1** | **NOT CHECKABLE.** `PASS1_RULINGS.md`, `F-8`, reports the history, but source contents cannot establish commit counts or changelog absence. Settling evidence: a permitted provenance artifact containing the scoped history and recorded pre-round search. |
| **2.2** | **DERIVED.** `bus_isa_init` snapshots the pointer before creating the 8253; `DEVINIT(8253)` publishes it afterward. `timer0_tick` increments the count, and reprogramming does not reset it. Reading the published machine pointer at acknowledge time avoids the stale snapshot. |
| **2.3** | **READ as a prior-source report, not independently verified guest code.** `PASS1_RULINGS.md`, `F-4`/`F-5`, contains the summarized guest sequences. The claimed source quotations at `F2_RULINGS 1.7/1.8` do not exist in that file. **NOT CHECKABLE:** their complete compatibility implications; a version-pinned guest-source extract would settle them. |
| **2.4** | **READ.** The census and synchronous UART deassertion agree with `bus_isa_init`, Malta�?Ts `MACHINE_SETUP`, and `dev_ns16550_access` calls to `dev_ns16550_tick`. |
| **2.5** | **DERIVED.** All three specified sketch repairs appear in the diff. �?oUnconverted machines behave as HEAD�?? needs qualification: the legacy EOI path preserves HEAD when that line has no modeled service bit. A guest that also polls can enter the changed path. |
| **2.6** | **READ.** The original witness has masked IRQ4 and poll value `0x84`; the revised detector separates the masked symptom from the unmasked control. |
| **2.7** | **READ.** The re-pin records 69/69 HEAD at **08:01:54**, before the reported FIX measurements. It was **after** the fix binary�?Ts recorded build at **07:56:45**. |
| **2.8** | **DERIVED, with one false statement.** The 65 Hz base and batching support rejecting the short-wait retirement oracle. However, �?onever fires�?? is literally wrong: `timer_clamp_freq` clamps zero to `TIMER_MIN_FREQUENCY`, `1e-8 Hz`. Programming zero also does not erase existing pending events. Anchors: `timer_add`, `timer_update_frequency`, `dev_8253_access`. |

**(i) DERIVED �?" `d->isr & bit` correctly tests the model�?Ts service bit, but the helper-only-setter argument proves less than claimed.** It records an acknowledge since the last clearing EOI; it does not establish complete guest service history.

Because equal-priority acknowledgements are permitted, a guest can acknowledge IRQ4 twice before completing the first service. One EOI clears the single ISR bit; a subsequent EOI takes the legacy branch even if an outer handler remains active. That is a consequence of the deferred `i8259priority` defect. Conversely, ICW4 AEOI is not retained�?"the existing arm only prints a warning�?"while the helper always sets ISR. The datasheet�?Ts `Automatic End of Interrupt` section makes that distinction explicit. Thus the discriminator is appropriate for the contained model, not an unconditional hardware invariant.

**(ii) READ �?" One-shot polling is supported by the datasheet�?Ts `Poll Command`: the next read acknowledges.** Neither reported Malta sequence requires a second poll byte without another OCW3: NetBSD issues a poll command for each poll; Linux uses GT IACK.

