(i) `d->isr & bit` is the right discriminator. READ. The only ISR writer in this tree is the helper (`|=`); `DEVINIT(8259)` zeroes the struct; both EOI arms only clear. There is no second "in service" flag. A guest reaches the legacy `else` only when that bit is clear, and the ISR arm only when the helper set it and no EOI has cleared it since. A second EOI of the same line correctly falls through to the legacy consume: the bit is not a counter. A specific EOI of a different line, while some other bit is set, correctly takes the legacy arm for the line that is not in service.

(ii) No supported guest wants the poll byte on a second read. DERIVED from F2 1.7/1.8 plus the datasheet. NetBSD `pcib_intr` writes `OCW3_POLL` and reads once per controller; the ISR/IRR branch is compiled out; attach writes `0x0a`. Linux malta never writes `0x0c`. Datasheet (`8259A.txt` poll command): the next RD after P=1 is the acknowledge and sets the IS bit. `8259A.txt` status-read paragraph: the chip remembers IRR versus ISR, "This is not true when poll is used," and polling overrides a status read only when P=1 and RR=1. `0x0c` is P=1, RR=0, so forcing `current_command = 0x0a` is the approximation F2 already flagged, and it matches NetBSD's attach selection. It is not a second poll byte.

