## Q3 -- The real diff

(a) The hybrid consume is the right contained form. READ. The legacy `else` already recalculates only when the unmasked set changes (`old_irr & ~d->ier` versus the value after `irr &= ~bit`). A masked bit is not in that set, so a masked legacy EOI does not touch the line. `masked eoi` pins the consume. No further guard is needed.

(b) Lowest-numbered is "highest priority" while rotation is unimplemented. READ. `8259A.txt`: after init, IR0 is highest and IR7 lowest; a non-specific EOI resets the highest IS bit of those set. `d->isr & -d->isr` is that lowest set bit (the `uint8_t` promotes before the negation; bit 7 clears cleanly). The rotate arms (`0xc0`�?"`0xc7` empty, `0xe0`�?"`0xe7` falling into the specific arm) do not implement rotation. `i8259ocw2nonspec` stays filed; it is not this diff.

(c) Two slave requests are not left pending with the CPU line low once the slave acknowledge runs. READ, walked.

Start: pic2 IRR has two unmasked bits, `isa_interrupt_common` has set pic1 bit 2, the CPU line is high. `pic8259_acknowledge(pic1)` takes n=2, clears pic1 bit 2, sets ISR bit 2, and `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(pic1->irq)` drops the CPU line while pic2's IRR is untouched. That gap is real and matches the chip (master INT falls at the acknowledge; edge mode will not re-latch IR2 until a new edge). No guest instruction runs inside `case GT_PCI0_INTR_ACK`. On the poll path NetBSD is already in `pcib_intr` and polls the slave next.

`pic8259_acknowledge(pic2)` then leaves one slave bit set and calls `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(pic2->irq)`. That object is the `isa.2` registration in `bus_isa_init`, so the handler is `isa_interrupt_assert`. The macro in `interrupt.h` calls the handler every time. `old_isa_assert` is sampled with pic1 bit 2 already clear, the assert ORs bit 2, and `isa_interrupt_common` sees a 0-to-nonzero mask and asserts `bus_isa`'s irq. The second request is presented. The F2 pre-fold (set bit 2 before the assert, so the mask does not change and `isa_interrupt_common` returns) is not in this diff.

A later slave tick before the slave poll also re-folds, because `isa_interrupt_assert` / `isa_interrupt_common` set pic1 bit 2 again whenever pic2 still has an unmasked request. The low window does not survive that tick.

(d) `pic1 == NULL` is reachable. `n2 < 0` is not, on a machine whose fold is intact. READ. `machine_new` memsets the machine. `MACHINE_MVMEPPC_5500` calls `dev_gt_init` and never `bus_isa_init`, so `isa_pic_data.pic1` stays NULL and the read returns `last_int`. `n2 < 0` would need pic1 bit 2 set while pic2 has no unmasked request. `isa_interrupt_common` clears bit 2 in that case on every ISA assert and deassert, and an OCW1 write that empties pic2's unmasked set `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT`s pic2's irq, which clears bit 2 too. PRETABLE's "unreachable short of a masking race" is right. The cell is still the correct total function: return 2, do not invent a slave line.

(e) A second line that arrives while one is in service is raised immediately, same as HEAD. READ. `isa_interrupt_assert` / `isa_interrupt_common` key the CPU line off `irr & ~ier` and never consult ISR. The helper's own recompute is the same test. Versus HEAD the new guest-visible shape is the acked line: its IRR is clear and, if it was the only request, the CPU line is low until something else asserts. A re-assert of that same line between acknowledge and EOI survives the specific EOI (`eoi acked`: FIX raw `0x10`). HEAD's EOI consumed it. That is the A10 disclosure (more generous than edge silicon, where a held level does not make a second edge). It is not a regression against HEAD, which had no in-service mask either. `i8259priority` stays open.

## Q4 -- Scope, records, landing

(a) Narrow, do not close. READ. `DEVICE_ACCESS(prep)` returns `last_int`. `DEVICE_ACCESS(footbridge_isa)` returns `irq_base + last_int`. Neither calls the helper, so ISR stays clear and the specific EOI takes the legacy consume: HEAD's behavior, which is the bug, preserved on purpose. `i8259isrnever` narrows to those readers (`isaiackreaders`). `dev_algor.c` still clears IRR itself (`algorisaintclear`); the hybrid does not fix that either.

(b) The exposure record is right, and MED WRONG-RESULT stays. DERIVED from the measured channels: legacy EOI consume (`eoi noack` / `eoi acked`), the 8253 storm (`rate storm` HEAD `0x1f38` = 7992, FIX `0x04` = 4, in `fixlegs_082756`), and the mask-blind poll (`poll masked` HEAD `0x84`, FIX `0x00`). Not a crash.

(c) File all of those except `i8259pollmask`, which the masked poll closes by construction if this lands. Keep `pit8253pendingptr`: `bus_isa_init`'s snapshot is still NULL even though the live retire moved. Add one detector row, not a new product row: a bare command-port read while a second unmasked IRR bit is set, so `M_sticky` returns a poll byte and the fix returns the raw IRR. The owed D_fold CPU-line row (two slave sources) is still owed; it is also the row that would see a GT read returning `last_int` after the in-read re-fold.

(d) Landing shape is right for a weekly battery. The equality check against 77 is the house idiom. Two legs at about 2.5 minutes is acceptable weekly; it is a poor fit for a per-commit hook. The rate band `1..1000` still passes a retire that is only roughly right. Hygiene pins and the manifest bump are records, not behavior.

(e) The re-pin is honestly ordered. The pass-1 witness is a wrong record if used as the fix-column oracle. READ. `witness_20261025b` (08:01, no fix binary) precedes `fixlegs_082756` (08:27). Pass 1 pinned `eoi irr4` / `eoi x8` as symptom rows that wanted the request kept after an un-acknowledged EOI. Under the hybrid those are `eoi noack` (both columns 0). Cite `witness_20261025b` as the oracle pin. Cite `witness_20261025` only as the superseded 19-row run.

## Q5 -- What was missed

`poll once` does not observe the one-shot, and the kill table's own sentence for `M_sticky` is false. The bare read was placed before the re-assert delay, the measured FIX value is `0x00` against an empty IRR, and `poll2` is not a row. A green `M_sticky` in the scores file is an escape, not a kill.

The same class of hole sits on the GT return. Every scored IACK value is equal to the `last_int` a single source already stored. The fix's `odata = n` is untested against "acknowledge, but hand back `last_int`." Linux would dispatch the surviving slave line. That wants the two-source row already owed for `D_fold`, scored on the IACK data, not only on the CPU line.
